                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "4 games, 4 mini-reviews for this recent, rather overshadowed collection:\n\n[b]Lure of the Temptress[/b]\n\nRevolution's first game was a bit embryonic, but still fun, and comforting in an old-school way. A fantasy piece, the titular temptress barely figures as a character at all, and for most of the game you're wandering round the tiny village of Turnvale, talking to NPCs and gathering items and information. Although the place is small, the fact that the villagers are constantly wandering around in their own way gives it a lived-in feel, and stops things from being too static or claustrophobic. Despite the lack of music (except in a few cutscenes), I warmed to the location, and the slow, calming pace of gameplay. There are a couple of obtuse moments towards the end of the game, but if you look at the \"Extras\" section, you'll find some scans of pages from a walkthrough, taken from a magazine of the time. Add a dollop of Pratchett-esque British humour and you've got a short, but enjoyable, nostalgic experience.\n\n[b]Beneath a Steel Sky[/b]\n\n[i]Beneath a Steel Sky[/i] is a masterpiece, and I've said all I can say about it in my review on the standalone version of the game, which I recommend over this Amiga version, and not just because that version is free on Steam. The standalone features superior audio quality, an opening cutscene, and doesn't cut back and forth between music and dialogue, as this version does. Still, it's a great game in any form, and I enjoyed replaying it for the umpteenth time.\n\n[b]Broken Sword: The Shadow of the Templars[/b]\n\nThird game packaged is the PlayStation version of [i]Broken Sword[/i], a lengthy point-and-click adventure taking place in Paris, with various stops around Europe and the Middle East along the way. It's another great game, one I hadn't played before, but perhaps people would prefer playing the \"Reforged\" version on Steam. It's more expensive, but the remastering I would assume means no loading screens or frame drops, with which this version is replete. You also get a version from 1996 bundled with that one, if you want a retro experience.\n \nTo come back to the version in this collection, most of the game is playable, but once I got to [spoiler]Syria[/spoiler], I was experiencing a lot of slow down, that made playing very frustrating for the length of time I was in the [spoiler]village of Marib[/spoiler]. Things cleared up once I travelled to the next location. Overall, maybe not the best way to play this classic, but still a classic.\n\n[b]Broken Sword II: The Smoking Mirror[/b]\n\nFinally, we get the Central American sequel, [i]Broken Sword II[/i], again, it's the PlayStation version. There weren't any slowdowns here, though I did get one crash (which weirdly, gave me an achievement before I'd satisfied its conditions), so save often. This is a game that is in the process of being \"Reforged\", and there's also a 2010 remaster available on Steam, so you have multiple options, to play, but this version was fine for me. One thing to note is that the PlayStation versions are quite pixelated, which can make \"pixel hunting\" rather difficult, and led to me resorting to a guide at one point (to find the [spoiler]maple syrup[/spoiler]).\n\n[b]Conclusion[/b]\n\n[i]Lure of the Temptress[/i] was the hidden gem for me, and I think this is the best version available. I do recall playing a free version on GOG.com years ago, but couldn't get far with it.\n\nThis package is also a decent way to dip your toes into the [i]Broken Sword[/i] series, because the \"Reforged\" version for the first [i]Broken Sword[/i] is really quite expensive, and a \"Reforged\" [i]Broken Sword II[/i] hasn't been released at the time of writing.\n\nFinally, if you only want [i]Beneath a Steel Sky[/i], just play the free version on Steam.\n \nAs for the extras: all achievements can be earned simply by playing through the four games, but having them does pad out the play experience, however artificially, and I like the art design of the achievement icons. The bonus features, manuals and trailers etc., are a nice little addition, but don't expect massive archives of content.\n\nFinal verdict: recommend, just know your options for accessing these games elsewhere and choose accordingly. Personally, I liked the varied, grab bag nature of this pack. And remember, in the words of the immortal Don Black, \"Happiness Is an Inside Job.\"",
